  {
   "n": 1,
   "topic": "Surds",
   "spec_refs": [
    "MM1.2"
   ],
   "difficulty": 2,
   "stem_html": "<p>What is the value of</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><span class=\"fr\"><span><span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span> + 2</span><span><span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span> \u2212 2</span></span> \u2212 <span class=\"fr\"><span><span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span> \u2212 2</span><span><span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span> + 2</span></span> ?</p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "8",
    "B": "4<span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span>",
    "C": "9 + 4<span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span>",
    "D": "18",
    "E": "8<span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span>"
   },
   "answer": "E",
   "solution_html": "<p>Rationalise: <span class=\"fr\"><span><span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span> + 2</span><span><span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span> \u2212 2</span></span> = <span class=\"fr\"><span>(<span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span> + 2)<sup>2</sup></span><span>5 \u2212 4</span></span> = 9 + 4<span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span>. Similarly <span class=\"fr\"><span><span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span> \u2212 2</span><span><span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span> + 2</span></span> = 9 \u2212 4<span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span>.</p><p>Difference = <span class=\"key\">8<span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\">5</span></span></span>.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "A": "8 drops the \u221a5 from 4\u221a5 \u2212 (\u22124\u221a5).",
    "B": "4\u221a5 is one of the two surd parts; the subtraction doubles it.",
    "C": "This is the first fraction alone.",
    "D": "18 is the sum of the two fractions, not the difference."
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 2,
   "topic": "Laws of indices",
   "spec_refs": [
    "MM1.1"
   ],
   "difficulty": 2,
   "stem_html": "<p>Solve</p><p style=\"text-align:center\">9<sup><span class=\"math\">x</span></sup> \u00d7 27<sup>1 \u2212 <span class=\"math\">x</span></sup> = <span class=\"fr\"><span>3<sup><span class=\"math\">x</span> + 4</sup></span><span>81</span></span></p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> = \u22121",
    "B": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> = <span class=\"fr\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span>",
    "C": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> = 1",
    "D": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> = 3",
    "E": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> = <span class=\"fr\"><span>7</span><span>2</span></span>"
   },
   "answer": "B",
   "solution_html": "<p>Everything in base 3. Left: 3<sup>2<span class=\"math\">x</span></sup> \u00d7 3<sup>3 \u2212 3<span class=\"math\">x</span></sup> = 3<sup>3 \u2212 <span class=\"math\">x</span></sup>. Right: 3<sup><span class=\"math\">x</span> + 4 \u2212 4</sup> = 3<sup><span class=\"math\">x</span></sup>.</p><p>3 \u2212 <span class=\"math\">x</span> = <span class=\"math\">x</span>, so <span class=\"key\">x = 3/2</span>.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "A": "Comes from 3 \u2212 x = x + 4 (forgetting to subtract the 4 from the 81).",
    "C": "Comes from 2x + 3 \u2212 3x = x with a sign slip giving 3 = 3x.",
    "D": "Comes from 3 \u2212 x = 0.",
    "E": "Comes from 3 \u2212 x = x \u2212 4."
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 3,
   "topic": "Roots of an equation with a parameter",
   "spec_refs": [
    "MM1.3"
   ],
   "difficulty": 4,
   "stem_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">k</span> is a real constant. For how many values of <span class=\"math\">k</span> does the equation</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><span class=\"math\">kx</span><sup>2</sup> + (<span class=\"math\">k</span> + 1)<span class=\"math\">x</span> + 1 = 0</p><p>have <strong>exactly one</strong> real solution?</p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "0",
    "B": "1",
    "C": "2",
    "D": "3",
    "E": "Infinitely many"
   },
   "answer": "C",
   "solution_html": "<p>If <span class=\"math\">k</span> \u2260 0 it is a quadratic with discriminant (<span class=\"math\">k</span> + 1)<sup>2</sup> \u2212 4<span class=\"math\">k</span> = (<span class=\"math\">k</span> \u2212 1)<sup>2</sup>. Exactly one (repeated) root when <span class=\"math\">k</span> = 1.</p><p>If <span class=\"math\">k</span> = 0 the equation is <span class=\"math\">x</span> + 1 = 0, which is linear and has exactly one solution.</p><p>So <span class=\"key\">2</span> values: <span class=\"math\">k</span> = 0 and <span class=\"math\">k</span> = 1.</p><p class=\"trap\">\"Exactly one real solution\" is not the same as \"repeated root\". The degenerate case k = 0 is the whole point.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "A": "Discriminant (k \u2212 1)\u00b2 is zero at k = 1.",
    "B": "k = 1 gives the repeated root, but k = 0 also gives one solution because the equation becomes linear.",
    "D": "There is no third value; (k \u2212 1)\u00b2 has one root.",
    "E": "(k \u2212 1)\u00b2 is positive for every k except 1, so the quadratic usually has two distinct roots."
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 4,
   "topic": "Quadratic inequalities",
   "spec_refs": [
    "MM1.5"
   ],
   "difficulty": 2,
   "stem_html": "<p>The complete set of values of <span class=\"math\">x</span> satisfying both <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> + <span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2212 6 \u2265 0 and <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> &lt; 4<span class=\"math\">x</span> is</p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "2 \u2264 <span class=\"math\">x</span> &lt; 4",
    "B": "2 &lt; <span class=\"math\">x</span> &lt; 4",
    "C": "0 &lt; <span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2264 2",
    "D": "<span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2264 \u22123 or <span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2265 2",
    "E": "\u22123 \u2264 <span class=\"math\">x</span> &lt; 0"
   },
   "answer": "A",
   "solution_html": "<p>(<span class=\"math\">x</span> + 3)(<span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2212 2) \u2265 0 gives <span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2264 \u22123 or <span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2265 2. <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> \u2212 4<span class=\"math\">x</span> &lt; 0 gives 0 &lt; <span class=\"math\">x</span> &lt; 4.</p><p>Both: <span class=\"key\">2 \u2264 x < 4</span>. The 2 is included because the first inequality is non-strict.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "B": "x = 2 satisfies both: 4 + 2 \u2212 6 = 0 \u2265 0 and 4 < 8.",
    "C": "Uses the region between the roots for the first inequality.",
    "D": "First inequality only.",
    "E": "Divided x\u00b2 < 4x by x without knowing its sign."
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 5,
   "topic": "Factor and remainder theorems",
   "spec_refs": [
    "MM1.6"
   ],
   "difficulty": 2,
   "stem_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">p</span>(<span class=\"math\">x</span>) = <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>3</sup> \u2212 2<span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> + <span class=\"math\">ax</span> + <span class=\"math\">b</span>. When <span class=\"math\">p</span>(<span class=\"math\">x</span>) is divided by (<span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2212 1) the remainder is 4, and (<span class=\"math\">x</span> + 1) is a factor of <span class=\"math\">p</span>(<span class=\"math\">x</span>).</p><p>What is the remainder when <span class=\"math\">p</span>(<span class=\"math\">x</span>) is divided by (<span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2212 2)?</p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "0",
    "B": "4",
    "C": "5",
    "D": "6",
    "E": "8"
   },
   "answer": "D",
   "solution_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">p</span>(1) = 1 \u2212 2 + <span class=\"math\">a</span> + <span class=\"math\">b</span> = 4, so <span class=\"math\">a</span> + <span class=\"math\">b</span> = 5. <span class=\"math\">p</span>(\u22121) = \u22121 \u2212 2 \u2212 <span class=\"math\">a</span> + <span class=\"math\">b</span> = 0, so <span class=\"math\">b</span> \u2212 <span class=\"math\">a</span> = 3.</p><p><span class=\"math\">b</span> = 4, <span class=\"math\">a</span> = 1. <span class=\"math\">p</span>(2) = 8 \u2212 8 + 2 + 4 = <span class=\"key\">6</span>.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "A": "Assumes (x \u2212 2) is also a factor.",
    "B": "Copies the remainder for (x \u2212 1).",
    "C": "a + b, not p(2).",
    "E": "Forgets the \u22122x\u00b2 term."
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 6,
   "topic": "Composite functions & domains",
   "spec_refs": [
    "MM1.7",
    "MM8.7"
   ],
   "difficulty": 4,
   "stem_html": "<p>f(<span class=\"math\">x</span>) = <span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\"><span class=\"math\">x</span></span></span> for <span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2265 0, and g(<span class=\"math\">x</span>) = <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> \u2212 4.</p><p>How many real values of <span class=\"math\">x</span> satisfy g(f(<span class=\"math\">x</span>)) = f(g(<span class=\"math\">x</span>)) ?</p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "0",
    "B": "1",
    "C": "2",
    "D": "3",
    "E": "4"
   },
   "answer": "A",
   "solution_html": "<p>g(f(<span class=\"math\">x</span>)) = <span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2212 4 for <span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2265 0. f(g(<span class=\"math\">x</span>)) = <span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\"><span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> \u2212 4</span></span>, defined for |<span class=\"math\">x</span>| \u2265 2.</p><p>Need <span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2212 4 = <span class=\"rt\">\u221a<span class=\"ov\"><span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> \u2212 4</span></span>. The right side is \u2265 0, so <span class=\"math\">x</span> \u2265 4. Squaring: <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> \u2212 8<span class=\"math\">x</span> + 16 = <span class=\"math\">x</span><sup>2</sup> \u2212 4, so <span class=\"math\">x</span> = 5/2.</p><p>But 5/2 &lt; 4, so it fails the sign condition (and indeed 5/2 \u2212 4 &lt; 0 while the square root is positive). <span class=\"key\">No solutions</span>.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "B": "x = 5/2 comes from squaring without checking that x \u2212 4 must be non-negative.",
    "C": "Counting \u00b15/2; neither works.",
    "D": "No route.",
    "E": "No route."
   }
  },
  {
   "n": 7,
   "topic": "Recurrence relations",
   "spec_refs": [
    "MM2.1"
   ],
   "difficulty": 2,
   "stem_html": "<p>A sequence satisfies <span class=\"math\">x</span><sub><span class=\"math\">n</span>+1</sub> = 2<span class=\"math\">x</span><sub><span class=\"math\">n</span></sub> \u2212 3 and <span class=\"math\">x</span><sub>4</sub> = 21.</p><p>What is <span class=\"math\">x</span><sub>1</sub>?</p>",
   "diagram_svg": null,
   "options": {
    "A": "3",
    "B": "<span class=\"fr\"><span>9</span><span>2</span></span>",
    "C": "5",
    "D": "<span class=\"fr\"><span>21</span><span>4</span></span>",
    "E": "12"
   },
   "answer": "D",
   "solution_html": "<p>Run backwards: <span class=\"math\">x</span><sub><span class=\"math\">n</span></sub> = (<span class=\"math\">x</span><sub><span class=\"math\">n</span>+1</sub> + 3)/2. <span class=\"math\">x</span><sub>3</sub> = 12, <span class=\"math\">x</span><sub>2</sub> = 15/2, <span class=\"math\">x</span><sub>1</sub> = <span class=\"key\">21/4</span>.</p>",
   "traps": {
    "A": "3 is the fixed point of the recurrence, not x\u2081.",
    "B": "9/2 comes from averaging 12 and \u22123 instead of adding 3 then halving.",
    "C": "Ran forwards instead of backwards.",
    "E": "This is x\u2083."
   }
